QB.add(5,[{"n":1,"q":"A 68-year-old man with a history of hypertension and diabetes presents to the emergency department with complaints of palpitations and shortness of breath for the past 2 hours. On examination, his pulse is irregularly irregular with a rate of 140 beats per minute. An ECG of the patient is given below. CHA2DS2-VASc score calculation shows a score of 3. Which of the following statements about his condition is FALSE?","o":[{"t":"The irregular pulse is due to abnormal atrial firing and re-entry circuits"},{"t":"The atrial rate is likely 300 beats per minute with a 2:1 AV block."},{"t":"Anticoagulation is recommended to reduce stroke risk."},{"t":"Beta-blockers and digoxin are the first-line rhythm and rate control agents respectively."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) The atrial rate is likely 300 beats per minute with a 2:1 AV block."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's clinical presentation such as palpitations, shortness of breath, irregularly irregular pulse and ECG findings like fibrillatory P waves, irregular QRS complexes are classic signs of atrial fibrillation (AF) ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"An atrial rate of 300 beats per minute with a 2:1 AV block is characteristic of atrial flutter , not atrial fibrillation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Atrial Fibrillat ion"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Features Characterized by abnormal automatic firing and multiple"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"re-entry circuits. (Option A ruled out) Atria beat rapidly and ineffectively; ventricles activated irregularly Produces an \"irregularly irregular\" pulse Palpitations, syncope, and breathlessness can be present Asymptomatic AF: Can present as systemic embolism and stroke"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Atrial Fibrillat ion"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ECG Feature QRS Complexes: Irregular but normal s P Waves: Absent; baseline may show irregular"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"fibrillation waves Ventricular Rate: Fast at onset (120-160 beats/min) Slower in chronic AF due to medication effects and AV nodal fatigue Fibrillation ECG features\" data-author=\"PeaBrainC\" data-hash=\"8584\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:At rial_Fibrillation_in_two_leads.jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"agwaGY41744292691.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Atrial Fibrillat ion"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Paroxysmal Atrial Fibrillation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"First-line Therapy: Beta-blockers are effective in patients with CAD, hypertension, and heart failure. (Option D ruled out) Class Ic Drugs: Propafenone or flecainide to prevent episodes (not for patients with CAD or LV dysfunction). Class III Drugs: Amiodarone : Most effective but with significant side effects; used when other treatments fail. Dronedarone : Alternative to amiodarone; contraindicated in heart failure. Rate Control: Digoxin and verapamil for AV node conduction slowing, but not for AF prevention. (Option D ruled out) Catheter Ablation: Considered for patients with drug-resistant AF; prevents AF in ~75% of cases but may have complications. Persistent Atrial Fibrillation Rhythm Control : Cardioversion : Electrical or pharmacological (e.g., intravenous flecainide or amiodarone). Timing: Immediate if AF <48 hours; anticoagulation required if AF ‡48 hours. Prophylactic Amiodarone : To reduce recurrence risk. Rate Control : Medications: Digoxin, b-blockers, or rate-limiting calcium antagonists (verapamil, diltiazem) . Combination Therapy: Digoxin with b-blockers ; avoid combining calcium antagonists with b-blockers. Pace and Ablate: Permanent pacemaker with AV nodal block for symptomatic AF. Thromboprophylaxis Anticoagulation: Required for stroke prevention due to blood stasis in the left atrium. Warfarin : INR target of 2.0–3.0; start 4 weeks prior to cardioversion. DOACs: Rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban, and dabigatran as alternatives to warfarin; no monitoring needed. Risk Assessment: CHA2DS2-VASc Score : For stroke risk; anticoagulate if score ‡2 . (Option C ruled out) HAS-BLED Score: For bleeding risk; monitor patients with score ‡3. Aspirin: Not recommended due to low efficacy in preventing embolic stroke."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CHA2DS2-VASc stroke risk scoring system for AF"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Points Parameters Score"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"C Congestive heart failure 1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"H Hypertension history 1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"A2 Age (‡75 years) 2"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"D Diabetes mellitus 1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"S2 Previous stroke or transient 2"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ischemic attack (TIA)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"V Vascular disease 1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"A Age (65-74 years) 1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sc Sex category (female) 1"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Maximum total score 9 points"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management option for AF as per CHA2DS2-VASc score"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"0: need not anticoagulate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"1: Give aspirin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":">=2: Warfarin/Dabigatran/Rivaroxaban"}],"qi":[3697]},{"n":2,"q":"A 68-year-old male patient with a history of hypertension presents to the emergency department in acute distress. He is diaphoretic, tachycardic, and complains of severe chest pain. His ECG is given below. Given the patient's condition, what is the most appropriate immediate management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Amiodarone"},{"t":"Radiofrequency catheter ablation"},{"t":"Digoxin"},{"t":"Synchronized cardioversion"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Synchronized cardioversion"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's clinical presentation of acute distress, diaphoresis, tachycardia, and severe chest pain, along with the ECG showing saw-tooth flutter waves, is indicative of Takotsubo cardiomyopath with hemodynamic instability. This necessitates immediate synchronized cardioversion to restore normal sinus rhythm and stabilize his condition."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Atrial Flutter"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Features Atrial flutter is characterized by a large re-entry circuit,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"usually within the right atrium, encircling the tricuspid annulus originating from the cavotricuspid isthmus. The atrial rate is approximately 300 beats per minute . It is typically associated with 2:1, 3:1, or 4:1 AV block, resulting in ventricular rates of 150, 100, or 75 beats per minute."},{"k":"img","v":3703},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ECG Featur es"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The ECG shows saw-tooth flutter waves , often referred to as \"F-waves.\" With a 2:1 AV block, flutter waves may be buried in QRS complexes and T waves, making them difficult to identify."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diagnosis Atrial flutter should be suspected in narrow-complex"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"tachycardia at a rate of 150 beats per minute. Carotid sinus pressure or intravenous adenosine may help reveal flutter waves by temporarily increasing the degree of AV block."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Atrial Flutter"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Manageme 1. Rhythm Control: nt Acute Management:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hemodynamically Unstable Patients: Synchronized cardioversion (Option D). Stable Patients: Electrical cardioversion (if no intracardiac thrombus). Pharmacological cardioversion: Antiarrhythmic drugs: Amiodarone (Option A ruled out) Class IA: Procainamide, Quinidine, Disopyramide Class IC: Flecainide, Propafenone Calcium Channel Blockers: Verapamil, Diltiazem Beta-Blockers: Metoprolol, Carvedilol, Esmolol Long-Term Management: Radiofrequency catheter ablation of the cavotricuspid isthmus. (Option B ruled out) 2. Rate Control: First-Line Agents: Calcium Channel Blockers (e.g., Verapamil, Diltiazem) Beta-Blockers (e.g., Metoprolol, Carvedilol, Esmolol) Digoxin (use cautiously due to toxicity). (Option C ruled out) Goal: Heart rate < 110 beats per minute. 3. Anticoagulation: Assess stroke risk using the CHADS2-VASC score: Anticoagulation Recommendations: 2 or more points: Strong recommendation for anticoagulation. 1 point: Consider either aspirin or full anticoagulation."}],"qi":[3703]},{"n":3,"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with fatigue, confusion, and muscle weakness. The ECG is attached below.  Which of the following would be the most likely diagnosis in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Hypokalemia"},{"t":"Hypercalcemia"},{"t":"Hypothyroidism"},{"t":"Hypomagnesemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: B) Hypercalcemia"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The combination of the patient's clinical presentation and the ECG finding of a shortened QT interval (Lead V3) strongly suggests hypercalcemia."},{"k":"img","v":3710},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The above ECG depicts a shortened QT interval"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"QT Interval Abnormalities:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Abnormali Causes ECG Findings ty"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prolonged Congenital Long QT Syndrome QTc > 440 ms in males or > 460 ms QT Interv Acquired causes: in females al - Electrolyte disturbances T wave abnormalities (broad,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, notched, or low amplitude) hypomagnesemia) (Option A and Increased risk of Torsades de Option D ruled out) Pointes - Medications (antiarrhythmics, antipsychotics, antibiotics) - Bradycardia - Hypothyroidism (Option C ruled out) - Hypothermia - Myocardial ischemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Shortened Congenital Short QT Syndrome QTc < 330 ms QT Interv Acquired causes: Tall, peaked T waves al - Hypercalcemia (Option B) Absent or very short ST segment"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Hyperkalemia Increased risk of atrial and - Hyperthermia ventricular arrhythmias - Acidosis - Digoxin effect (not toxicity) - Increased sympathetic tone"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Abnormali Causes ECG Findings ty"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"QT Disper Myocardial ischemia or infarction Difference between the longest and sion Left ventricular hypertrophy shortest QT interval > 50 ms across"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Electrolyte imbalances all 12 leads Autonomic dysfunction Associated with increased risk of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ventricular arrhythmias"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"T Wave Severe heart disease Beat-to-beat alternation in T wave Alternans Electrolyte imbalances amplitude, morphology, or polarity"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Long QT syndrome May be visible or microscopic Acute myocardial ischemia (requiring special ECG processing)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Associated with increased risk of sudden cardiac death"}],"qi":[3710]},{"n":4,"q":"A 45-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with confusion and muscle weakness. His laboratory results show a serum potassium level of 7.2 mEq/L. Which of the following T-wave morphologies is most likely to be seen on this patient's ECG?","o":[{"t":""},{"t":""},{"t":""},{"t":""}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: In the setting of severe hyperkalemia (serum potassium > 7.0 mEq/L), the ECG typically shows peaked/tall T-waves, which is represented in Option C."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"T-wave abnormalities:"},{"k":"img","v":3798},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"T-wave Abnormality Associated Conditions and Characteristics"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Peaked T-waves Hyperkalemia: Narrow, pointed, and tall T-waves"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hyperacute phase of myocardial infarction: Broad-based, asymmetric T-waves Left ventricular hypertrophy: Tall T-waves in precordial leads Prinzmetal angina: Tall, pointed T-waves during chest pain Normal variant: Especially in V2-V4 in young patients and athletes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Inverted T-waves Myocardial ischemia: Symmetrically inverted T-waves"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Wellens syndrome: Deeply inverted or biphasic T-waves in V2-V4 Cerebrovascular injury: Deep, symmetrically inverted T-waves (cerebral T-waves) Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Deep T-wave inversions in lateral precordial leads Pulmonary embolism: T-wave inversion in III, aVF (as part of S1Q3T3 pattern)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Flattened T-waves Hypokalemia: Flattened T-waves, sometimes with"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"prominent U waves Hypothyroidism: Low amplitude, flattened T-waves Digoxin effect: Flattened or inverted T-waves with ' scooped' ST segments"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Other T-wave changes Left bundle branch block: T-wave discordant to QRS"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"complex Right bundle branch block: T-wave inversion in right precordial leads Acute pericarditis: T-wave inversion following ST-segment elevation Hypomagnesemia: Widened T-waves with flattened tops"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Flattened T-waves (Option A) are more commonly associated with hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Deeply inverted T-waves (Option B) are seen in conditions like myocardial ischemia or Wellens syndrome, not hyperkalemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Biphasic T-waves (Option D) can be seen in Wellens syndrome or as a normal variant, but are not characteristic of hyperkalemia."}],"m":1},{"n":5,"q":"A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with chest pain. His ECG is given below.  Which of the following is NOT a common cause of changes seen in the ECG above?","o":[{"t":"Acute myocardial infarction"},{"t":"Digitalis effect"},{"t":"Left ventricular hypertrophy"},{"t":"Hypokalemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG shows ST elevation in leads I, AVL, and V2-V5 & reciprocal depressions in the inferior leads (II, III, AVF). This is a case of acute anterior wall infarction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemia is typically associated with ST-segment depression , not elevation, thus being the correct choice in this scenario."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of ST-Segment Elevation and Depression"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ST elevation ST depression"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute myocardial infarction (Option A) Myocardial ischemia Early repolarization (normal variant) Hypokalemia (Option D ruled out) Pericarditis Digitalis effect (Option B ruled out) Left ventricular hypertrophy Reciprocal changes in acute MI Left bundle branch block Left ventricular hypertrophy (Option C ruled out) Ventricular aneurysm Left bundle branch block"}],"qi":[3716]},{"n":6,"q":"A 62-year-old man presents to the emergency department with palpitations and lightheadedness. His ECG shows a rapid, regular rhythm with a rate of 150 beats per minute as shown below  Which of the following conditions is most likely to be associated with this ECG finding?","o":[{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia"},{"t":"Wolf-Parkinson-White syndrome"},{"t":"Hypothermia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: B) AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The ECG description provided- rapid, regular rhythm with narrow QRS complexes and no discernible P waves - is consistent with a narrow QRS complex tachycardia ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Narrow QRS complex tachycardia is associated with AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia or AV reentrant tachycardia (Orthodromic) ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Orthodromic: The Action Potential propagates in the physiological direction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antidromic: The Action Potential propagates in the opposite direction to the physiological direction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types of QRS Complex Abnormalities:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type of QRS Complex Associated Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Narrow QRS complex AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia, AV reentrant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"tachycardia (Orthodromic)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Broad QRS complex Ventricular tachycardia, Torsades de Pointes Type of QRS Complex Associated Conditions"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Delta Wave Wolf-Parkinson-White syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"J wave/Osbourne wave Hypothermia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Epsilon wave Arrhythmogenic right ventricular dysplasia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventricular tachycardia (Option A) is associated with broad QRS complexes, not narrow ones as described in the question."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wolf-Parkinson-White syndrome (Option C) is associated with a delta wave on ECG, which wasn't mentioned in the description. While it can cause a type of narrow complex tachycardia (orthodromic AVRT), it's not the most likely diagnosis based on the information given."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypothermia (Option D) is associated with J waves (Osbourne waves) on ECG, which were not described in the question. It also doesn't typically cause tachycardia."}],"qi":[3722]},{"n":7,"q":"Match the following tachyarrhythmias with their most appropriate initial treatment: Tachyarrhythmia Treatment Atrial Fibrillation A. Intravenous Adenosine Stable Monomorphic Ventricular Tachycardia B. Synchronized Cardioversion Torsades de Pointes C. Intravenous Magnesium Sulfate Supraventricular Tachycardia (AVNRT) D. Intravenous Amiodarone\nTachyarrhythmia Treatment\nAtrial Fibrillation A. Intravenous Adenosine\nStable Monomorphic Ventricular Tachycardia B. Synchronized Cardioversion\nTorsades de Pointes C. Intravenous Magnesium Sulfate\nSupraventricular Tachycardia (AVNRT) D. Intravenous Amiodarone","o":[{"t":"1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A"},{"t":"1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"},{"t":"1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C"},{"t":"1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: A. 1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of Tachyarrhythmias:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Condition Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation Esmolol/ Ibutilide"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atrial flutter Esmolol/ Ibutilide"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT)/PSVT Prevention: Verapamil"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Unstable: Adenosine"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wolff Parkinson White (WPW) syndrome Prevention: Flecainide"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Drug of choice: Procainamide"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventricular tachycardia Post MI: Lignocaine"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Stable: Amiodarone"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventricular bigeminy Lignocaine Condition Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Torsades de Pointes (TDP) Magnesium sulphate"}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with palpitations and lightheadedness. She has a history of hypertension and is on multiple medications. Her ECG is given below:  Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Administer intravenous amiodarone"},{"t":"Perform immediate electrical cardioversion"},{"t":"Administer intravenous magnesium sulfate"},{"t":"Start oral beta-blocker therapy"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: C. Administer intravenous magnesium sulfate"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG is suggestive of Torsades de Pointes (TdP) , a specific form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia occurring in the setting of QT interval prolongation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous magnesium sulfate is the first-line treatment for TdP, as it helps suppress the triggering PVCs and can shorten the QT interval."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Torsades de Pointes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polymorphic VT : Characterized by variation in QRS direction and amplitude."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spindle-shaped pattern : The QRS complexes form a twisting or spindle-shaped \"envelope.\""},{"k":"img","v":3807},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolonged QT interval"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PVC initiation : Often triggered by a premature ventricular contraction (PVC) on a prolonged T-U wave, creating an \"R on T\" phenomenon."},{"k":"img","v":3813},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Postectopic pause : A pause following the PVC, further prolonging repolarization."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mnemonic: \"A TIGERS BITE\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A - Antiarrhythmics (Class IA and III)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T - Tricyclic antidepressants"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"I - Ischemia (myocardial)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"G - Genetics (congenital long QT syndrome)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"E - Electrolyte abnormalities (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypocalcemia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"R - Restrictive cardiomyopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S - Stroke (especially subarachnoid hemorrhage)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B - Bradycardia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"I - Interactions (drug-drug interactions that prolong QT)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T - Toxins (organophosphates, arsenic, etc.)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"E - Endocrine disorders (hypothyroidism, pheochromocytoma)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immediate management: Intravenous magnesium sulfate (even if serum magnesium is normal)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Discontinue potential QT-prolonging medications"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Correct electrolyte abnormalities, especially hypokalemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Consider temporary pacing or isoproterenol infusion to increase heart rate and shorten QT interval"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Amiodarone (Option A) should be avoided as it can further prolong the QT interval."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immediate electrical cardioversion (Option B) is usually reserved for hemodynamically unstable patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oral beta-blockers (Option D) are not the immediate management, although they may be considered later in the treatment course."}],"qi":[3728]},{"n":9,"q":"A 65-year-old male with a history of prior myocardial infarction presents to the emergency department with severe palpitations, dizziness, and chest pain. His blood pressure is 80/50 mmHg, and he appears diaphoretic and confused. An ECG shows wide QRS complexes, a regular rhythm, and a ventricular rate of 160 bpm. What is the most appropriate initial treatment for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Intravenous Adenosine"},{"t":"Intravenous Magnesium Sulfate"},{"t":"Synchronized Cardioversion"},{"t":"Intravenous Procainamide"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: C. Synchronized Cardioversion"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient has unstable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia (VT) as indicated by the wide QRS complexes, regular rhythm, and associated symptoms (hypotension, chest pain, altered mental status). Hemodynamic instability in VT requires immediate synchronized cardioversion , which delivers a shock timed with the R wave to avoid precipitating ventricular fibrillation (VF)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathology: VT is defined as three or more consecutive premature ventricular contractions (PVCs) with a heart rate of over 100 bpm. It can be either monomorphic (all QRS complexes appear similar) or polymorphic (QRS complexes vary in shape)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Monomorphic VT is often related to a reentry circuit around a myocardial scar, typically after myocardial infarction, while polymorphic VT, especially Torsades de Pointes, is related to prolonged QT intervals, ischemia, or electrolyte imbalances."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ECG Findings:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monomorphic VT : Wide QRS complexes (>120 ms), regular rhythm, and consistent morphology across leads. The QRS axis may vary depending on the site of origin (e.g., LBBB or RBBB-like morphology)."},{"k":"img","v":3734},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polymorphic VT : Continuously changing QRS morphology, often associated with prolonged QT intervals (in Torsades de Pointes)."},{"k":"img","v":3820},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stable Monomorphic VT : Intravenous procainamide or amiodarone can be used. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unstable Monomorphic VT : Immediate synchronized cardioversion is required. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polymorphic VT (Torsades de Pointes) : IV magnesium sulfate and correction of underlying electrolyte abnormalities (e.g., hypokalemia) are crucial, along with discontinuation of QT-prolonging drugs. (Option B ruled out)"}],"qi":[3734]},{"n":10,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome: WPW syndrome is characterized by the presence of an accessory pathway between the atria and ventricles. The classic ECG findings in WPW syndrome include a prolonged PR interval, delta waves, and narrow QRS complexes. Orthodromic AVRT in WPW syndrome always presents with a wide QRS complex tachycardia. A premature ventricular complex (PVC) can initiate AVRT in patients with WPW syndrome. Which of the following options correctly identifies the true statements?","o":[{"t":"1 and 4 only"},{"t":"2 and 3 only"},{"t":"4 only"},{"t":"1, 2, 3, and 4"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: A. 1 and 4 only"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome is characterized by the presence of an abnormal accessory electrical pathway between the atria and ventricles, bypassing the AV node (Statement I). This accessory pathway, also known as the Bundle of Kent, allows for premature ventricular activation during sinus rhythm, resulting in characteristic ECG findings:"},{"k":"img","v":3826},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig: ECG in WPW Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A premature ventricular complex (PVC) occurring close to the ventricular entrance of the accessory pathway can initiate AVRT in patients with WPW syndrome (Statement IV)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atrioventricular Reentrant Tachycardia (AVRT)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AVRT is a type of supraventricular tachycardia that occurs in patients with an accessory pathway, such as in Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AVRT involves a reentrant circuit that includes the normal AV conduction system and the accessory pathway."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Two types exist: Orthodromic AVRT (more common): Conduction travels down the AV node and up the accessory pathway. Antidromic AVRT (less common): Conduction travels down the accessory pathway and up the AV node."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthodromic AVRT (more common): Conduction travels down the AV node and up the accessory pathway."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antidromic AVRT (less common): Conduction travels down the accessory pathway and up the AV node."},{"k":"img","v":3829},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ECG Features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthodromic AVRT: Narrow QRS complex tachycardia (QRS < 120 ms) (Statement III ruled out) Regular rhythm with heart rates typically 150-250 bpm Retrograde P waves often visible in the ST segment or early T wave"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Narrow QRS complex tachycardia (QRS < 120 ms) (Statement III ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular rhythm with heart rates typically 150-250 bpm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Retrograde P waves often visible in the ST segment or early T wave"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antidromic AVRT: Wide QRS complex tachycardia (QRS > 120 ms) Regular rhythm with heart rates typically 170-240 bpm Retrograde P waves may be obscured by the wide QRS"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wide QRS complex tachycardia (QRS > 120 ms)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular rhythm with heart rates typically 170-240 bpm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Retrograde P waves may be obscured by the wide QRS"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Narrow QRS complex tachycardia (QRS < 120 ms) (Statement III ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular rhythm with heart rates typically 150-250 bpm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Retrograde P waves often visible in the ST segment or early T wave"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wide QRS complex tachycardia (QRS > 120 ms)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular rhythm with heart rates typically 170-240 bpm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Retrograde P waves may be obscured by the wide QRS"},{"k":"img","v":3832},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AVRT can be initiated by premature atrial or ventricular complexes that block one pathway and conduct through the other, setting up the reentrant circuit."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 25-year-old female presents with sudden onset palpitations. ECG is taken and given below. It shows a narrow complex tachycardia at 180 bpm. The postgraduate successfully terminates the arrhythmia using the Valsalva maneuver. Which of the following best describes the primary mechanism by which this maneuver terminates the arrhythmia?","o":[{"t":"Direct stimulation of the sinoatrial node"},{"t":"Increased sympathetic tone to the heart"},{"t":"Transient block in AV node conduction"},{"t":"Mechanical disruption of accessory pathways"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: C. Transient block in AV node conduction"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Valsalva maneuver transiently increases intrathoracic pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This leads to a reflex increase in vagal tone (parasympathetic activity)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased vagal tone affects the AV node, potentially causing a transient block in conduction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Effect on the arrhythmia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Many supraventricular tachycardias involve a reentry circuit that includes the AV node."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A transient block in AV node conduction can disrupt this circuit."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This disruption can terminate the tachyarrhythmia, often restoring normal sinus rhythm."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig: Typical AVNRT terminated by carotid sinus massage"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal Supraventricular Tachycardia (PSVT):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sudden onset of rapid, regular palpitations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dizziness or lightheadedness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shortness of breath"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest discomfort or pressure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anxiety"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fatigue"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In severe cases: syncope, hypotension, or heart failure symptoms"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular, narrow-complex tachycardia (QRS duration <120 ms)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced R-R interval"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart rate between 150-250 bpm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P waves often not visible or buried in T waves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common types include: a) AV Nodal Reentrant Tachycardia (AVNRT): Retrograde P waves may appear as pseudo R' in V1 or pseudo S in inferior leads b) AV Reentrant Tachycardia (AVRT): Retrograde P waves may be visible after the QRS complex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"a) AV Nodal Reentrant Tachycardia (AVNRT): Retrograde P waves may appear as pseudo R' in V1 or pseudo S in inferior leads"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b) AV Reentrant Tachycardia (AVRT): Retrograde P waves may be visible after the QRS complex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"a) AV Nodal Reentrant Tachycardia (AVNRT): Retrograde P waves may appear as pseudo R' in V1 or pseudo S in inferior leads"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b) AV Reentrant Tachycardia (AVRT): Retrograde P waves may be visible after the QRS complex"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Acute termination: Vagal maneuvers (e.g., Valsalva, carotid sinus massage)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"If vagal maneuvers fail, IV adenosine is the first-line pharmacological treatment: 6 mg - 12mg fast iv push. Alternative drugs: verapamil, diltiazem, or beta-blockers Synchronized cardioversion for hemodynamically unstable patients"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Long-term managem Catheter ablation: Often curative, especially for AVNRT ent: and AVRT"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pharmacological therapy: Beta-blockers or calcium channel blockers for prevention Class IC or III antiarrhythmic drugs in selected cases Patient education on performing vagal maneuvers"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lifestyle modification Avoid triggers (e.g., caffeine, alcohol, stress) s: Regular exercise"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Adequate sleep"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Direct stimulation of the sinoatrial node (Option A): While vagal maneuvers can affect the SA node, this is not the primary mechanism for terminating this type of arrhythmia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased sympathetic tone to the heart (Option B): Vagal maneuvers decrease sympathetic tone."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mechanical disruption of accessory pathways (Option D): Vagal maneuvers do not physically disrupt anatomical pathways."}],"qi":[3740]},{"n":12,"q":"A 50-year-old man comes to your clinic for a routine check-up. He has no significant past history, exercises regularly, and reports no chest pain, palpitations, or shortness of breath. On physical examination, his blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg, and his pulse rate is 75 beats per minute and regular. You perform an ECG as part of the screening, shown below.  What is the most likely finding based on this ECG?","o":[{"t":"Left ventricular hypertrophy"},{"t":"Normal sinus rhythm"},{"t":"First-degree AV block"},{"t":"Right bundle branch block"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: B. Normal sinus rhythm"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Normal ECG:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Com Cause Normal Duration and Size pone nt"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"P wa Atrial depolarization is initiated by 0.08–0.11 seconds ve the SA node. 2–2.75 small boxes"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"<2.5 mm (0.25 mV) in amplitude/ height <0.12 sec (120 msec or 3 small boxes) in width"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"PR in Time from onset of atrial 0.12–0.20 seconds terval depolarization (P wave) to onset of 3–5 small boxes"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ventricular depolarization (electrical impulse passing through AV node)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Q wa Initial depolarization of the <0.04 seconds ve interventricular septum, directed <1 small box"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"from left to right. Depth: <1/4 of R wave or ~1–2 mm"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(<0.5 small boxes)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"QRS Ventricular depolarization - the time <0.10 seconds comp taken for the electrical impulse to <2.5 small boxes in width lex spread through the ventricles. Height: 5–30 mm (1–6 big boxes)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"QT in Time from the start of ventricular Duration depends on HR. terval depolarization to the end of HR inversely proportional QT"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ventricular repolarization. length. Rate-corrected (QTc)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"330–350 msec ~11 small boxes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ST s Represents the early part of 0.08–0.12 seconds egm ventricular repolarization. It is 2–3 small boxes ent isoelectric (flat). Normally isoelectric (flat), deviations"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":">1 small box indicate pathology"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"T wa Represents the later stage of <0.04 seconds ve ventricular repolarization. 2.5–6.25 small boxes"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Height: Varies. Normally <5 mm (1 big box) in limb leads, <10 mm (2 big boxes) in precordial leads"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"U wa Unknown origin; possibly related to Not always measurable ve repolarization of Purkinje fibers or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ventricular walls."},{"k":"img","v":3841},{"k":"img","v":3846},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH) (Option A) presents with larger-than-normal QRS voltages, especially in the precordial leads (V1–V6), and may show left axis deviation. This ECG shows normal QRS amplitude, ruling out LVH."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"First-degree AV block (Option C) is characterized by a prolonged PR interval (>0.20 seconds). Since the PR interval is normal (0.16 seconds), a first-degree AV block is not present."},{"k":"img","v":3852},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Right bundle branch block (RBBB) (Option D) is identified by a prolonged QRS duration (>0.12 seconds) with an \"M-shaped\" QRS in leads V1 and V2. This ECG shows a normal QRS duration, so RBBB is unlikely."}],"qi":[3746]},{"n":13,"q":"A 70-year-old patient is diagnosed with severe bradycardia and is scheduled for a pacemaker implantation. The electrophysiologist is evaluating different types of artificial pacemakers. Where are the leads of the single-lead and dual-lead pacemakers placed in the heart?","o":[{"t":"Single-lead: Right ventricle; Dual-lead: Right atrium and right ventricle"},{"t":"Single-lead: Right atrium; Dual-lead: Right ventricle and left ventricle"},{"t":"Single-lead: Left ventricle; Dual-lead: Right atrium and left ventricle"},{"t":"Single-lead: Right ventricle; Dual-lead: Left ventricle and right ventricle"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Single-lead: Right ventricle; Dual-lead: Right atrium and right ventricle"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The single-lead pacemaker has its lead placed in the right ventricle, while the dual-lead pacemaker has leads in the right atrium and right ventricle."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pacemakers of the Heart:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Natural Pacemaker sites and their depolarization rates:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sinoatrial node: 60 to 100 beats per minute"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atria: less than 60 beats per minute"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrioventricular node: 40 to 60 beats per minute"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ventricles: 20 to 40 beats per minute"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Artificial Pacemaker:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"An artificial pacemaker is a small battery-operated device that helps regulate the heart's rhythm by producing electrical impulses. It typically consists of three parts: a generator, leads (wires), and sensors (electrodes). Traditional pacemakers are implanted under the skin, usually on the chest just below the collarbone or in the stomach area, and they connect to the heart through tiny wires."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"There are several types of traditional pacemakers:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wireless pacemakers are smaller and consist of a pulse generator and electrodes in one device. They are implanted inside the heart through a catheter inserted via internal jugular or femoral or subclavian vein, sending impulses to the right ventricle without the need for traditional surgery."},{"k":"img","v":3855}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 74-year-old patient comes to the emergency department with complaints of intermittent dizziness and lightheadedness. An electrocardiogram (ECG) is performed, as shown below. Based on the ECG findings and the clinical scenario, what type of atrioventricular block is most likely present in this patient?","o":[{"t":"2:1 AV block (Type I - Wenckebach)"},{"t":"2:1 AV block (Type II - Infranodal)"},{"t":"First-degree AV block"},{"t":"Third-degree AV block"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) 2:1 AV block (Type II - Infranodal)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2:1 AV Block (Type II - Infranodal) is identified by the presence of every other P wave not followed by a QRS complex (As seen in the ECG), with a normal PR interval and a prolonged QRS complex. This pattern confirms the Type II block, which typically indicates a conduction defect in the His-Purkinje system."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"2:1 Atrioventricular Block"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"About 2:1 AV block occurs when every other P wave is not"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"conducted to the ventricles, resulting in missed QRS complexes. It can arise from either the AV node (nodal) or the His-Purkinje system (infranodal)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Types Nodal (Type I - Wenckebach): Results from impaired"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"conduction in the AV node, which can be influenced by sympathetic and parasympathetic tone. Characterized by progressively prolonged PR intervals before a dropped beat; usually benign. Infranodal Block (Type II): Indicates a conduction defect in the His-Purkinje system, where interventions affecting the AV node will not alter the block. It is less affected by autonomic interventions. PR interval remains normal with a dropped beat; often requires a permanent pacemaker."},{"k":"img","v":3862},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ECG Features"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Every other P wave is not followed by a QRS complex. In Type I, the PR interval is progressively prolonged, and the QRS complex is narrow. In Type II, the PR interval is normal, and the QRS complex is prolonged. A narrow QRS with Type I suggests AV nodal block, while a wide QRS indicates infranodal involvement."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnostic Carotid Sinus Massage/Adenosine: Slows the sinus Maneuvers rate, potentially unmasking PR interval changes"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"indicative of Type I. Atropine Administration: Enhances AV conduction; can resolve Type I block. Exercise Testing: Also enhances AV conduction, potentially eliminating Type I block."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Type II block typically requires implantation of a"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"permanent pacemaker. In cases of His-Purkinje disease, atropine may worsen the degree of block."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2:1 AV Block (Type I - Wenckebach) (Option A): This type is characterized by progressively lengthening PR intervals before a dropped beat. In this case, the PR interval is normal, and there is no evidence of progressive prolongation, ruling out Type I."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"First-Degree AV Block (Option C): First-degree AV block is characterized by a prolonged PR interval (>0.20 seconds) but maintains a consistent relationship between P waves and QRS complexes. In this scenario, there is a lack of consistent conduction, making first-degree block unlikely."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Third-Degree AV Block (Option D): This condition shows complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes, with no correlation between the two. While there are missed QRS complexes, the normal PR interval suggests a Type II block rather than complete dissociation, making third-degree AV block an incorrect diagnosis."}],"qi":[3752]},{"n":15,"q":"A 72-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe fatigue, dizziness, and episodes of syncope. His medical history includes hypertension and a prior myocardial infarction. ECG of the patient is given below. What is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Oral beta-blockers"},{"t":"Intravenous atropine"},{"t":"Permanent pacemaker placement"},{"t":"Observation only"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Intravenous atropine"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Based on the symptoms of severe fatigue, dizziness, syncope, and the ECG findings of complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes, the diagnosis is a third-degree atrioventricular block."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The initial management for third-degree AV block may include intravenous atropine to attempt to increase heart rate, but it is often ineffective in this condition."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Third-Degree Atrioventric ular Block"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathology Complete loss of communication between the atria and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ventricles. SA node activity continues independently, but impulses do not reach the ventricles. Atrial and ventricular activities are completely dissociated."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Etiology Common causes include idiopathic fibrosis, structural"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"heart disease, acute ischemia, medication toxicity, and post-surgical effects. Associated conditions: Lyme disease, collagen vascular disorders, and myocardial infarction (especially inferior wall MI). Risk factors include diabetes, hypertension, and prior cardiovascular diseases."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Third-Degree Atrioventric ular Block"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features Symptoms may include fatigue, chest pain, shortness of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"breath, and syncope. Patients often exhibit bradycardia and may show signs of hemodynamic instability. Physical exam may reveal cannon A-waves due to simultaneous atrial and ventricular contractions."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ECG Features Characterized by complete atrioventricular dissociation."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The atrial rate is faster than the ventricular rate. ECG shows independent P waves and QRS complexes with no relationship between them. P-P interval and R-R interval is constant but there is no synchrony between P-R. Atrioventricular Block ECG features\" data-author=\"James Heilman, MD\" data-hash=\"8599\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wi kipedia.org/wiki/Third-degree_atrioventricular_block\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com /notes/D6nk8lt9Rq1xAHWpLGoA1744293577.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Initial management may include intravenous atropine ."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Patients typically require pacing, either temporary (transcutaneous or transvenous) or permanent pacemaker placement. Urgent consultation with cardiology and electrophysiology is advised for further management."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oral beta-blockers (Option A) : These are contraindicated in the setting of third-degree AV block, as they can further decrease heart rate."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Permanent pacemaker placement (Option C) : This is typically required for definitive treatment but is not the immediate management in an emergency setting."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Observation only (Option D) : This is insufficient for a patient with symptomatic third-degree AV block, as urgent intervention is necessary."}],"qi":[3758]},{"n":16,"q":"A 68-year-old male patient presents to the emergency department with complaints of fatigue and occasional lightheadedness. He has a history of hypertension and coronary artery disease. During the examination, he describes episodes of near-syncope. The physician performs an ECG to evaluate his symptoms. What is the most likely diagnosis based on the ECG findings?","o":[{"t":"First-degree heart block"},{"t":"Mobitz Type I"},{"t":"Mobitz Type II"},{"t":"Complete heart block"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Mobitz Type II"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In Mobitz Type II second-degree AV block, there is a consistent PR interval followed by a non-conducted P wave, leading to dropped beats. This type is often associated with structural heart disease and can progress to complete heart block, which aligns with the patient’s symptoms and history."},{"k":"img","v":3870},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Second-degree AV block:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Second-degree AV block is characterized by a delay in conduction between the atria and ventricles, leading to a prolonged PR interval."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Two types exist: Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) and Mobitz Type II."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) Mobitz Type II"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathology Progressive prolongation of PR Constant PR interval until a P wave"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"interval until dropped beat. is not followed by a QRS complex. PR interval longest before dropped beat."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Etiology Normal variant; high vagal tone; Associated with structural heart"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"inferior ischemia; medication disease; myocardial ischemia; toxicity; post-surgery. fibrosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Often asymptomatic; Symptoms include fatigue, dyspnea, Features May cause syncope or chest pain;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"lightheadedness. Can progress to complete heart"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"block."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) Mobitz Type II"},{"k":"img","v":3876},{"k":"img","v":3881},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ECG Feat ures"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"PR interval progressively lengthens"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Constant PR interval;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"before a dropped beat ."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"May have widened QRS complex."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatmen Usually no treatment needed; may Requires immediate pacing; often t use atropine if symptomatic. necessitates a permanent"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pacemaker."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"First-Degree AV Block (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized by a consistently prolonged PR interval (greater than 200 ms) but with every P wave conducted to the ventricles."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There are no dropped beats, and the relationship between the P waves and QRS complexes remains intact."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While the PR interval may be prolonged in this case, the presence of a dropped QRS complex indicates a higher degree of block, making this option incorrect for the given ECG findings."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mobitz Type I (Option B):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In Mobitz Type I, there is a progressive lengthening of the PR interval until a QRS complex is dropped."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This differs from the scenario where the PR interval remains constant. The patient’s ECG does not show this progressive change, ruling out this type of block."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complete Heart Block (Option D):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a complete heart block, there is a complete dissociation between atrial and ventricular activity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P waves may occur regularly, but QRS complexes do not follow them in a predictable pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While the patient may exhibit symptoms similar to those in Mobitz Type II, the ECG findings suggest a consistent PR interval rather than complete dissociation, making this option unlikely."}],"qi":[3764]},{"n":17,"q":"A 58-year-old woman comes to the clinic for a routine follow-up. She has a history of mild hypertension, which is well-controlled with medication. She reports no symptoms such as palpitations, dizziness, or chest pain. An ECG of the patient is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis based on the ECG findings?","o":[{"t":"Second-Degree AV Block"},{"t":"First-Degree AV Block"},{"t":"Third-Degree AV Block"},{"t":"Bundle Branch Block"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) First-Degree AV Block"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The ECG shows a prolonged PR interval (>0.20 seconds) but consistently conducts P waves to QRS complexes, indicating first-degree AV block. There are no dropped beats, ruling out higher-degree blocks."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"First Degree AV Block"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathology First-degree AV block is characterized by a prolonged"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"PR interval (>0.20 seconds) without disruption of conduction from atria to ventricles. The delay typically occurs in the AV node, but may also involve the atria, His bundle, or Purkinje fibers."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Etiology Commonly attributed to increased vagal tone in younger"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"patients. In older patients, often due to fibrotic changes in the cardiac conduction system. Other causes include coronary heart disease, myocardial infarction, electrolyte abnormalities, inflammation, infections, certain drugs, infiltrative diseases, collagen vascular diseases, and idiopathic degenerative diseases. Lymes disease, Chagas disease, Diphtheria, Rheumatoid arthritis, Scleroderma."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features Usually asymptomatic; patients are often unaware of the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"condition until discovered on an ECG. In cases where the PR interval exceeds 0.30 seconds, symptoms may resemble \"pacemaker syndrome,\" including dyspnea, malaise, lightheadedness, and chest pain."},{"k":"img","v":3884},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ECG Features"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diagnosis is confirmed by a PR interval greater than 0.20 seconds on an ECG. PR interval can be marked (>0.30 seconds) in some cases, and P waves may appear within preceding T waves."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"First Degree AV Block"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Generally, no treatment is required; routine observation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"is sufficient. Pacemaker placement may be considered for symptomatic patients with PR interval >0.30 seconds or those with coexisting heart disease. No antiarrhythmic medications are indicated, and surveillance with routine ECGs is recommended to monitor for worsening."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Second-Degree AV Block (Option A): This block features progressive lengthening of the PR interval followed by a dropped QRS complex, which is absent in this ECG as there are no dropped beats."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Third-Degree AV Block (Option C): Here, here is complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes. This is absent in the ECG since P waves are consistently followed by QRS complexes."},{"k":"img","v":3890},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bundle Branch Block (Option D): This condition shows a wide QRS complex with characteristic morphology. This feature is absent in the ECG, where the QRS complexes are normal in width and shape."}],"qi":[3770]},{"n":18,"q":"A 25-year-old male presents with recurrent palpitations and dizziness. An ECG of the patient is given below. Which of the following statements is true regarding this patient's diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"He is at risk for pre-excited atrial fibrillation leading to dangerously fast ventricular rates"},{"t":"The accessory pathway is the Bundle of James, facilitating rapid conduction."},{"t":"The ECG shows a short PR interval, normal QRS, and no delta wave"},{"t":"Adenosine is contraindicated in this condition"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) He is at risk for pre-excited atrial fibrillation leading to dangerously fast ventricular rates."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Syndrome , characterized by recurrent palpitations and dizziness due to an accessory pathway causing abnormal conduction. The ECG typically shows a short PR interval, wide QRS complex, and a delta wave, indicating pre-excitation of the ventricles."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In WPW Syndrome, the presence of an accessory pathway can lead to pre-excited atrial fibrillation, which poses a significant risk of very rapid ventricular rates, potentially leading to syncope or other complications."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspe Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Lown-Ganong-Levine (LGL) ct Syndrome Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Patho Accessory pathways (APs) known LGL is a type of paroxysmal logy as the Bundle of Kent are the supraventricular tachycardia (SVT)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"abnormal tissue bands connecting characterized by a short PR interval the atria and ventricles. (Option B and a normal QRS duration. ruled out) It involves an accessory pathway They allow conduction outside the called the Bundle of James that AV node, either antegrade or allows rapid conduction between the retrograde (in concealed APs), atria and ventricles, often linked to bypassing the normal AV the atrioventricular (AV) node. conduction system. Accessory pathway bypasses the Present from birth, they result from normal pathway and connects to incomplete partitioning of the atrium BOH. and ventricle by the fibrous AV rings. Associated with Ebstein’s anomaly, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, PRKAG2 mutations, Danon’s disease, and Fabry’s disease. Accessory pathway connects to ventricular myocardium."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinic Common arrhythmias include Patients may experience episodes al Fe narrow-complex paroxysmal of palpitations, dizziness, or ature supraventricular tachycardia syncope. s (PSVT), wide-complex tachycardias, Symptoms can vary in frequency"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and rarely, sudden death. and intensity; some may have mild Tachycardia may develop due to symptoms while others experience re-entry circuits, causing significant distress. orthodromic or antidromic AV Episodes may be triggered by re-entry tachycardias (AVRT). stress, caffeine, or exertion. Pre-excited atrial fibrillation (AF) can occur, leading to a dangerously fast ventricular rate, syncope, or collapse, requiring urgent treatment."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspe Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Lown-Ganong-Levine (LGL) ct Syndrome Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ECG Short PR interval (<0.12 s), wide Short PR interval (typically less than Featu QRS complex, and delta wave 120 ms). res (slurred upstroke of the QRS). Normal QRS complexes (narrow"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option C ruled out) QRS). QRS and delta wave morphology The absence of a delta wave (unlike depend on the AP location: in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome). Right-sided AP: LBBB pattern (lead May show tachycardia during V1). episodes of SVT, often with a Left-sided AP: RBBB pattern (lead regular rhythm. V1), negative delta wave in aVL (can mimic lateral wall infarction). Posteroseptal AP: Negative delta wave in leads III and aVF (mimicking inferior wall infarction). Preexcitation may disappear during exercise."},{"k":"img","v":3773},{"k":"img","v":3897},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treat Acute management: Carotid sinus Acute Management: ment pressure or intravenous adenosine Vagal maneuvers (e.g., Valsalva"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"can terminate the tachycardia. maneuver) can terminate SVT (Option D ruled out) episodes. For pre-excited AF, urgent DC Adenosine may be used for acute cardioversion is recommended due termination of tachycardia. to the risk of collapse or death. Chronic Management: Definitive treatment: Catheter Beta-blockers or calcium channel ablation is first-line and curative in blockers may be prescribed for most cases. prevention. Medications: Flecainide or Catheter ablation can be considered propafenone can be used as in cases of recurrent or symptomatic alternatives but are less preferred. episodes. Avoid digoxin, verapamil, and amiodarone due to adverse effects on AP conduction."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspe Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Lown-Ganong-Levine (LGL) ct Syndrome Syndrome"},{"k":"img","v":3903},{"k":"img","v":3905}],"qi":[3773]},{"n":19,"q":"A 28-year-old male presents to the emergency department with palpitations and lightheadedness. He has no significant past medical history and is otherwise healthy. After a thorough examination, an ECG is obtained.  Based on the patient’s presentation and the findings of the ECG, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Atrial Fibrillation"},{"t":"Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Syndrome"},{"t":"First-degree AV Block"},{"t":"Myocardial Infarction"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome is a type of pre-excitation syndrome caused by an accessory pathway (the Bundle of Kent) that allows electrical impulses to bypass the normal conduction system of the heart."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This can lead to a characteristic presentation, including symptoms like palpitations, lightheadedness, and potentially syncope."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of a short PR interval and a delta wave on the ECG indicates ventricular pre-excitation, typical of WPW syndrome. This condition can predispose individuals to tachyarrhythmias."},{"k":"img","v":3773},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"PR Interval :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The PR interval measures the time from the beginning of the P wave (atrial depolarization) to the beginning of the QRS complex (ventricular depolarization)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Normal PR Interval"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Range: 0.12 to 0.20 seconds (120 to 200 milliseconds)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A normal PR interval indicates proper conduction through the AV node."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Short PR Interval"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A PR interval of less than 0.12 seconds (120 milliseconds)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated Conditions: Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Syndrome: Characterized by a short PR interval, wide QRS complex, and delta wave, indicating ventricular pre-excitation due to an accessory pathway. Ectopic Pacemaker Activity: A short PR interval with retrograde P waves (negative in lead II) suggests an ectopic pacemaker from the atria or AV junction. Atrial Fibrillation: No PR interval is present due to chaotic atrial depolarization. Atrial Flutter: A standard PR interval is absent due to continuous, rapid flutter waves instead of distinct P waves. These flutter waves occur at rates of 250–350 beats per minute, creating a \" sawtooth\" ECG pattern. The AV node conducts only some impulses (e.g., 2:1 or 4:1), leading to an organized ventricular response but without a conventional PR interval."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Syndrome: Characterized by a short PR interval, wide QRS complex, and delta wave, indicating ventricular pre-excitation due to an accessory pathway."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ectopic Pacemaker Activity: A short PR interval with retrograde P waves (negative in lead II) suggests an ectopic pacemaker from the atria or AV junction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial Fibrillation: No PR interval is present due to chaotic atrial depolarization."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial Flutter: A standard PR interval is absent due to continuous, rapid flutter waves instead of distinct P waves. These flutter waves occur at rates of 250–350 beats per minute, creating a \"sawtooth\" ECG pattern. The AV node conducts only some impulses (e.g., 2:1 or 4:1), leading to an organized ventricular response but without a conventional PR interval."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Syndrome: Characterized by a short PR interval, wide QRS complex, and delta wave, indicating ventricular pre-excitation due to an accessory pathway."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ectopic Pacemaker Activity: A short PR interval with retrograde P waves (negative in lead II) suggests an ectopic pacemaker from the atria or AV junction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial Fibrillation: No PR interval is present due to chaotic atrial depolarization."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial Flutter: A standard PR interval is absent due to continuous, rapid flutter waves instead of distinct P waves. These flutter waves occur at rates of 250–350 beats per minute, creating a \"sawtooth\" ECG pattern. The AV node conducts only some impulses (e.g., 2:1 or 4:1), leading to an organized ventricular response but without a conventional PR interval."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation (AF) (Option A) is characterized by an irregularly irregular rhythm and the absence of distinct P waves on the ECG. While patients with WPW syndrome can develop atrial fibrillation, the question describes a patient with palpitations and a more stable rhythm pattern, making this option less likely."},{"k":"img","v":3909},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"First-degree AV block (Option C) is characterized by a prolonged PR interval (>200 ms) with a regular rhythm. The patient in the scenario has a short PR interval, which does not align with this diagnosis."},{"k":"img","v":3915},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myocardial infarction (MI) (Option D) usually presents with specific changes on the ECG, including ST-segment elevation or depression and T-wave inversions, depending on the type of MI. The symptoms of palpitations and lightheadedness without additional signs of ischemia or infarction make this option unlikely."}],"qi":[3773]},{"n":20,"q":"A 34-year-old woman presents with progressive fatigue, palpitations, and mild dyspnea on exertion. She has a known history of a congenital heart condition diagnosed in childhood but has not had regular follow-ups. On examination, there is a prominent jugular venous pulse and a soft systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border. A 12-lead ECG was done and it is given below.  What is the most likely cause of the abnormal P waves on her ECG?","o":[{"t":"Ebstein’s Anomaly"},{"t":"Hyperkalemia"},{"t":"Atrial flutter"},{"t":"Mitral stenosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Answer: A) Ebstein’s Anomaly"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ebstein's anomaly is a congenital heart defect characterized by the Downward placement of tricuspid valves leading to atrialization of the Right ventricle and right atrial enlargement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this condition, the right atrium can become significantly dilated, causing the P waves on the ECG to appear broad and tall."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given shows the findings of broad and tall P waves along with a prolonged PR interval and a right bundle branch block pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This presentation aligns well with the patient's symptoms of fatigue and palpitations and is supported by the examination findings of jugular venous distention."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"P Wave in ECG:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Normal P Wave Right Atrial Enlargement ( Left Atrial Enlargement ("},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"P Pulmonale ) P Mitrale )"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cause Normal atrial Pulmonary disease Mitral valve disease"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"depolarization Congenital heart disease Left ventricular failure"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Tricuspid valve disorders"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Amplitude < 2.5 mm > 2.5 mm (in leads II, III, May be increased but less"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"aVF, sometimes V1) prominent than in RAA"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Duration < 0.12 sec (120 ms) Normal (< 0.12 sec) Prolonged > 0.12 sec"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(broad P wave)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Shape Smooth, upright Tall, peaked, narrow Notched/bifid (\"M\"-shaped)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"in lead II"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lead II Appear Smooth and Tall, peaked P wave Notched/bifid P wave ance positive"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lead V1 Biphasic (small Deep P wave Biphasic with prominent, Appearance negative) prolonged negative"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"terminal"},{"k":"img","v":3918},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hyperkalemia (Option B) , or elevated potassium levels, can cause changes in the ECG, resulting in peaked T waves, widened QRS complexes , and potentially a flattening or disappearance of the P waves . The presence of broad and tall P waves in this patient contradicts the expected ECG changes seen in hyperkalemia, making this option unlikely."},{"k":"img","v":3921},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atrial flutter (Option C) is characterized by a rapid reentrant circuit in the atrium originating from the cavotricuspid isthmus that produces a characteristic sawtooth pattern of P waves (F-waves) on the ECG, usually in a regular rhythm."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral stenosis (Option D) often leads to left atrial enlargement , which typically presents with bifid P waves (known as \"P mitrale\") on the ECG. While it can cause pulmonary hypertension and secondary right heart effects, the broad and tall P waves in this patient do not suggest mitral stenosis. Instead, the right atrial enlargement associated with Ebstein’s anomaly fits better with the ECG findings."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"stenosis Bifid P wave\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8440\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"440\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/Bb7GI5vf6n8241dD 7nhh1744280802.png\" width=\"440\" />"}],"qi":[3780]},{"n":21,"q":"A 60-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and diabetes presents with a one-month history of mild exertional dyspnea. She denies chest pain but has noticed some occasional palpitations. Physical examination reveals a blood pressure of 140/90 mmHg and regular heart sounds. You order an ECG, which is shown below.  What is the most likely diagnosis based on this ECG?","o":[{"t":"Normal axis"},{"t":"Right axis deviation"},{"t":"Left axis deviation"},{"t":"Extreme axis deviation"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The ECG in question shows left axis deviation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"QRS is POSITIVE (dominant R wave) in Lead I"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"QRS is NEGATIVE (dominant S wave) in leads II, III and aVF"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"QRS Axis: This refers to the mean orientation of the QRS vector in the frontal plane, which determines how electrical activity flows through the heart."},{"k":"img","v":3924},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Normal Axis : QRS axis between -30° and +90°, points downward and slightly left."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Abnormal Axis Deviations:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Normal QRS Axis: (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ranges from -30° to +100° . Variations within this range are considered normal."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"On ECG, this shows a positive QRS complex in both lead I and aVF."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Left Axis Deviation (LAD):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Defined when the QRS axis is more negative than -30°."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tall R wave in lead I, deep S wave in the lead III, and biphasic RS complex in lead II (with the S wave deeper than the R wave)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common causes: Left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH) Left anterior fascicular block (left anterior hemiblock) Inferior myocardial infarction (MI)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left anterior fascicular block (left anterior hemiblock)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inferior myocardial infarction (MI)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can also occur as a normal variant."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left anterior fascicular block (left anterior hemiblock)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inferior myocardial infarction (MI)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Right Axis Deviation (RAD): (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Defined when the QRS axis is more positive than +90° to +100° ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"R wave in lead III is taller than lead II, RS complex in lead I (S wave deeper than R wave), and tall R waves in inferior leads (II, III, aVF)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common causes: Right ventricular overload (acute or chronic) Lateral myocardial infarction Left posterior fascicular block Dextrocardia Left pneumothorax Electrode misplacement (reversal of left and right arm electrodes)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Right ventricular overload (acute or chronic)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lateral myocardial infarction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left posterior fascicular block"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dextrocardia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left pneumothorax"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Electrode misplacement (reversal of left and right arm electrodes)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"May occur as a normal variant, especially in children and young adults."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Right ventricular overload (acute or chronic)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lateral myocardial infarction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left posterior fascicular block"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dextrocardia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left pneumothorax"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Electrode misplacement (reversal of left and right arm electrodes)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lead Normal Axis Left Axis Deviation (LAD) Right Axis Deviation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(RAD)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lead I Positive (R wave > S Positive (R wave > S Negative (S wave > R"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"wave) wave) wave)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lead II Positive (R wave > S Negative (S wave > R Positive (R wave > S"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"wave) wave) wave)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lead III Positive (R wave > S Negative (S wave > R Positive (R wave > S"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"wave) or isoelectric wave) wave)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lead aVF Positive (R wave > S Negative (S wave > R Positive (R wave > S"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"wave) wave) wave)"},{"k":"img","v":3931},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Extreme Axis Deviation: (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A rare condition where QRS complexes are negative in both lead I and aVF ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated with ventricular tachycardia or hyperkalemia"}],"qi":[3786]}]);
